{
 "title": "Splitting the variation: a demonstration board",
 "intro": "In a paper written at this station, R. A. Fisher and W. A. Mackenzie grew a dozen potato varieties under several different manures and asked whether every variety answered the manures alike. To find out, they split the total sum of squares into parts: the manures, the varieties, the varieties' differing response to the manures, and the noise, the scatter between plots treated the same. Each part was set against the noise, to see whether it beat chance. That table of parts is the analysis you are running today. The board below works the same split on a small demonstration trial, three varieties on dunged and undunged rows, not on the paper's own figures.",
